# Hearth — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local for local development. On Railway, set these as service
# variables. Hearth has NO database; this file is the entire configuration
# surface. Nothing here is a NEXT_PUBLIC_* value — none of it reaches the
# browser bundle. Every secret is read server-side only.

# ── Device auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The long-lived device token. The wall is authorized once by visiting
#   https://<app>/setup?token=<HEARTH_DEVICE_TOKEN>
# which sets an httpOnly cookie that persists. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32     (or: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))")
HEARTH_DEVICE_TOKEN=

# ── Phase 1 / 1.5 — Google Calendar ────────────────────────────────────────
# One dedicated household Google account holds access to all four family
# calendars (spec §5.1). Run the OAuth consent flow once as that account and
# capture the refresh token — one token, not four. All are read only here,
# server-side, and never reach the browser.
#
# Phase 1.5 turns Hearth into a read-WRITE client for event creation only
# (spec D2, amended). Grant the household account "Make changes to events" on
# every calendar that should appear in the Add Event picker, and re-consent with
# the write scope: add https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events and drop
# calendar.readonly (the write scope covers reading). Then capture a new refresh
# token and revoke the old grant.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN=
#
# MEMBERS — the canonical list of taggable people (Phase 1.5). JSON array of
# { key, name, color }. `key` is a stable lowercase slug written into an event's
# hearthMembers tag; `color` is a palette slug (maryann | mitchell | lincoln |
# ollie, or a new one you add to globals.css) resolving to var(--color-<slug>).
# Mitchell is locked to green (spec D3). Single line, compact JSON:
# MEMBERS=[{"key":"maryann","name":"Maryann","color":"maryann"},{"key":"mitchell","name":"Mitchell","color":"mitchell"},{"key":"lincoln","name":"Lincoln","color":"lincoln"},{"key":"ollie","name":"Ollie","color":"ollie"}]
#
# CALENDAR_MAP — Google calendar id -> { label, writable, defaultMemberKey? }.
# `label` shows in the Add Event picker; `writable:true` makes the calendar a
# valid write target AND lists it in the picker (the write path allowlists on
# this — a calendar absent here, or writable:false, is rejected); `defaultMemberKey`
# is the fallback color for untagged events on that calendar (the Phase 1
# behavior). There may be more calendars than people. Single line, compact JSON:
# CALENDAR_MAP={"cal-1@group.calendar.google.com":{"label":"Maryann","writable":true,"defaultMemberKey":"maryann"},"cal-2@...":{"label":"Mitchell","writable":true,"defaultMemberKey":"mitchell"}}
#
# HOUSEHOLD_TIMEZONE — IANA name (e.g. America/New_York). Google requires an
# explicit timezone on event creation and will not infer it. Defaults to the
# server's own timezone if unset, but set it explicitly on Railway.
# HOUSEHOLD_TIMEZONE=America/New_York
#
# Local dev only — never set on Railway. Serves deterministic synthetic events so
# the calendar UI can be exercised without real Google credentials. Ignored in
# production builds even if set.
# HEARTH_CALENDAR_MOCK=1

# ── Phase 2 — Shared settings (customizable colors) ─────────────────────────
# HEARTH_DATA_DIR — an absolute path on a PERSISTENT disk where Hearth stores its
# shared settings (currently the customizable family colors, #3) as settings.json.
# On Railway, attach a Volume to the service and set this to its mount path (e.g.
# /data); every device then reads the same colors. If unset, color settings fall
# back to in-process memory — they work but reset on restart and aren't shared
# (fine for local dev).
# HEARTH_DATA_DIR=/data

# ── Phase 2 · 4 — Tada! (Clean & Chores) ───────────────────────────────────
# Reads: Clean session (next task by decay, optional room), room list, today's
# completions, kids' chores. Write: task completion for ANY household member —
# kids' chores AND Maryann's session — scoped to complete_task, source="hearth"
# (spec §5.2–5.3). This is a broader grant than kids-only; acceptable only because
# the wall is a trusted in-home device (§5.3, §6.3).
#
# IMPORTANT: the current Tada! backend does NOT yet expose these device-scoped
# endpoints (no inbound device token, source is a closed enum without "hearth",
# no acting-member override). Hearth's client targets the contract spelled out in
# docs/tada-integration-requirements.md; until Tada! ships it, run locally with
# HEARTH_TASKS_MOCK=1 and the live views degrade to a calm "not connected" state.
#
# TADA_API_URL      Base URL of the Tada! API (Hearth calls ${TADA_API_URL}/api/hearth/*).
# TADA_DEVICE_TOKEN Static device token, sent as a Bearer header, scoped to the
#                   reads above + complete_task/undo. Never reaches the browser.
# TADA_API_URL=
# TADA_DEVICE_TOKEN=
#
# TADA_MEMBERS — the people the wall may complete tasks as (the acting-member
# allowlist; a completion naming anyone else is rejected 403). JSON array of
# { memberKey, tadaUserId, role }: `memberKey` links to MEMBERS above for name +
# color (one color system across the app), `tadaUserId` is that person's Tada!
# user id, `role` is "adult" or "kid" (kids get a Chores column). Single line:
# TADA_MEMBERS=[{"memberKey":"maryann","tadaUserId":"<id>","role":"adult"},{"memberKey":"lincoln","tadaUserId":"<id>","role":"kid"},{"memberKey":"ollie","tadaUserId":"<id>","role":"kid"}]
#
# HEARTH_ADULT_ID — Maryann's Tada! user id: whose session the Clean view is (its
# completions are attributed to this id) and whose completions done-today shows.
# Must match a role:"adult" entry in TADA_MEMBERS.
# HEARTH_ADULT_ID=
#
# Local dev only — never set on Railway. Serves deterministic synthetic tasks,
# rooms, and chores so Clean & Chores can be exercised without the real Tada! API
# (the session advances, done-today fills, undo reverses, all in dev-server
# memory). Ignored in production builds even if set.
# HEARTH_TASKS_MOCK=1
#
# Phase 4 adds two more Tada! surfaces, both shown in the Shopping view: the
# supplies roster (read) and flag-low (write). Flagging a supply low is ONE Tada!
# call; Tada!'s backend adds it to the Enchanted Spoon shopping list — Hearth
# makes no Enchanted Spoon write for it. Same token (spec §4.6, §5.3, Q8).

# ── Phase 4 — Enchanted Spoon (not used yet) ───────────────────────────────
# The meal planner (earlier drafts called it "MealGenie"). Meals is read-only
# (current-week plan + tap-to-open meal cards); Shopping is read-write (check,
# add, remove, edit list items). Enchanted Spoon is the single source of truth;
# Hearth keeps no copy. One token, scoped server-side to the shopping-list read +
# item-mutation endpoints and the read-only meal plan/cards (spec §5.4–5.5, D6).
# ENCHANTED_SPOON_API_URL=
# ENCHANTED_SPOON_DEVICE_TOKEN=
